{
  "dom_id": "step-q149604",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Given the joint probability distribution for two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ below, find $\\text{E}[X+6Y].$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the marginal distribution for $X$ corresponds to the row totals, and the marginal distribution for $Y$ corresponds to the column totals.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore,  the probability mass functions for  $X$ and $Y$ are given by the following tables:\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">${f}_{X}(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"50\"></th>\n<th>\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n\n<tbody><tr> \n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n\n<tr>\n<td width=\"30\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\">${f}_{Y}(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.35$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nNow, we can compute the expected values for $X$ and $Y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\,{f}_{X}({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.45+4\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =0.9+2.2 \\\\  & =3.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}\\,{f}_{Y}({y}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.25+1\\cdot 0.15+2\\cdot 0.35+3\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =0+0.15+0.7+0.75 \\\\  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the properties of expectation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X+6Y] & =\\text{E}[X]+6\\,\\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.1+6(1.6) \\\\  & =3.1+9.6 \\\\  & =12.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
